1
00:00:02,420 --> 00:00:05,120
Hello English learners, welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:05,700
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:06,660
And I'm Erica.

4
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:10,240
And we're bringing you the final lesson of our job interview series.

5
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:10,900
That's right.

6
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:17,860
And in this lesson, we're going to be focusing on two very, very common, but sometimes difficult questions.

7
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:22,500
Okay, so we've talked about preparing for the job interview in part one.

8
00:00:23,060 --> 00:00:26,960
Part two, we talked about educational background and work experience.

9
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:29,760
And in part three, what are we going to be looking at?

10
00:00:30,050 --> 00:00:35,290
We're going to be looking at two questions, why we should hire you and your greatest weakness.

11
00:00:35,770 --> 00:00:41,510
Okay, so these are the classic common questions that you'll get at a job interview, right?

12
00:00:41,810 --> 00:00:42,130
That's right.

13
00:00:42,430 --> 00:00:48,350
So why don't we just get started and we'll take a listen to the dialogue and see how our interviewee handles these questions.

14
00:00:57,830 --> 00:00:58,450
Very good.

15
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:01,400
Now, I have a couple of final questions.

16
00:01:01,450 --> 00:01:02,370
I hope they're not too hard.

17
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:07,650
Well, why should we hire you?

18
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:11,230
I think that I would be a perfect fit in this company.

19
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:16,590
I have a unique combination of strong technical acumen with outstanding soft skills.

20
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:21,460
You know, I excel at building strong, long-term consumer relationships.

21
00:01:21,470 --> 00:01:27,530
For example, when I headed the customer support department in my previous company, our team resolved about 70% of our customers' problems.

22
00:01:28,330 --> 00:01:32,590
I decided that we needed better information and technical preparation on our products.

23
00:01:33,410 --> 00:01:40,850
So after I implemented a series of training sessions in coordination with our technical department, we were able to solve 90% of our customers' issues.

24
00:01:41,390 --> 00:01:44,970
Given the opportunity, I could bring this kind of success to this company.

25
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:47,760
Impressive.

26
00:01:49,530 --> 00:01:52,470
So, what would you consider to be your greatest weakness?

27
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:57,400
To be honest, I struggle with organization and time management.

28
00:01:57,400 --> 00:01:59,560
Punctuality has never been a strength of mine.

29
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:01,240
I find it hard to organize my time effectively.

30
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:07,320
I've actually addressed this weakness recently by attending a workshop on efficient time management.

31
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:14,040
It helped me a lot by providing me with great insights on how to get organized and use my time efficiently.

32
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:16,980
So, I think I'm getting better now.

33
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:19,560
Great.

34
00:02:21,450 --> 00:02:24,870
Well, let me tell you that I am very pleased with this interview.

35
00:02:25,530 --> 00:02:27,370
We are shortlisting our candidates this week.

36
00:02:27,690 --> 00:02:34,390
And next week, we will inform our shortlisted candidates of the day and time for a second interview with our CEO.

37
00:02:35,290 --> 00:02:35,670
Great.

38
00:02:35,910 --> 00:02:36,350
Thanks a lot.

39
00:02:36,830 --> 00:02:37,670
I hope to hear from you.

40
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,040
Goodbye.

41
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:54,840
Well, our interviewee is doing very well, but I think he had a little bit too many beans or chili.

42
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:55,200
Oh my God.

43
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:58,760
Like, this is probably the most embarrassing thing I can think of.

44
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:00,300
But he doesn't say anything.

45
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:01,320
So, I don't know.

46
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:04,640
Maybe he should have said, oh, pardon me, excuse me or something.

47
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:05,260
I don't know.

48
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:06,640
I don't know.

49
00:03:06,640 --> 00:03:07,640
It's a controversy.

50
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:08,640
Yeah.

51
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:09,640
Okay.

52
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:11,600
But what isn't a controversy is the language because there's some great stuff in here.

53
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:12,600
All right.

54
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:14,840
Let's take a look at the language in Language Takeaway.

55
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:18,170
Language Takeaway.

56
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:20,840
All right.

57
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:30,750
We've got some great phrases and words for you to use when you're talking about what sort of benefits and skills and experience and expertise you can bring to a company.

58
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:32,520
Okay.

59
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:37,770
The first thing that he said, and it's a very interesting word, he said, technical acumen.

60
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:38,910
Technical acumen.

61
00:03:39,480 --> 00:03:39,950
Acumen.

62
00:03:40,390 --> 00:03:40,710
Acumen.

63
00:03:41,510 --> 00:03:42,730
This is a really hard word.

64
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:44,440
What is acumen?

65
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:53,430
It's basically insight and understanding and the ability to make good and difficult decisions.

66
00:03:53,870 --> 00:03:54,210
Okay.

67
00:03:54,250 --> 00:03:57,650
So, he has a very strong technical acumen.

68
00:03:57,990 --> 00:04:01,550
So, a strong insight or understanding of technical issues.

69
00:04:01,850 --> 00:04:02,110
Okay.

70
00:04:02,370 --> 00:04:07,110
Or I can change it and may say, I have a very strong business acumen.

71
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:07,710
Exactly.

72
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:13,160
So, these are the two most common ways that this word is used, technical acumen, business acumen.

73
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:14,160
Okay.

74
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:15,160
Great.

75
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:18,270
Now, let's take a look at another word which is very interesting because everybody knows it.

76
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:20,320
Yes.

77
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:21,320
Excel.

78
00:04:21,320 --> 00:04:22,320
Excel.

79
00:04:22,710 --> 00:04:23,290
Excel.

80
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:24,320
Excel.

81
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:26,320
Now, Excel is, of course, the software that you use, right?

82
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:27,320
Yeah.

83
00:04:27,320 --> 00:04:30,170
But here, it's a verb meaning to be really, really good at something.

84
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:31,800
Right.

85
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:32,830
To be the best maybe.

86
00:04:33,280 --> 00:04:34,280
Yeah.

87
00:04:34,280 --> 00:04:35,860
To excel at something.

88
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:36,220
Yes.

89
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:39,040
So, I excel at cooking.

90
00:04:39,560 --> 00:04:39,920
All right.

91
00:04:40,220 --> 00:04:41,700
So, Excel, you are the best.

92
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:45,980
That's not really going to help me in a job interview, but the next word might.

93
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:47,600
Head.

94
00:04:48,040 --> 00:04:49,040
Head.

95
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:52,760
Well, in the dialogue, we heard it as headed in the past, right?

96
00:04:52,920 --> 00:04:53,920
Yeah.

97
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:54,920
That's right.

98
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:56,280
He headed the customer support department.

99
00:04:56,480 --> 00:05:03,490
So, I think this is a great word to use when you were saying, I was the leader.

100
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:06,630
I was the main person in charge.

101
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:07,450
Yeah, the manager.

102
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:13,640
So, this is a nice verb to use to show that you were leading something.

103
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:14,640
Okay.

104
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:14,770
Okay.

105
00:05:15,640 --> 00:05:16,170
Now, for the next word.

106
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:18,720
Struggle.

107
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:19,720
To struggle.

108
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:20,720
Struggle.

109
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:21,720
Struggle.

110
00:05:21,720 --> 00:05:23,700
So, what does struggle mean?

111
00:05:23,700 --> 00:05:25,530
It means that you have difficulty doing something.

112
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:27,770
So, it's hard for you.

113
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:29,120
Yeah.

114
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:33,390
For example, when I was in school, I struggled with math.

115
00:05:33,790 --> 00:05:34,030
Okay.

116
00:05:34,210 --> 00:05:36,730
And now, I struggle with Chinese.

117
00:05:37,670 --> 00:05:38,190
Exactly.

118
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:40,320
So, to have a hard time.

119
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:41,320
Yeah.

120
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:43,470
Why don't we hear a couple of examples of how this word is used?

121
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:49,640
Example one.

122
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:51,860
We're really struggling to reach our sales target.

123
00:05:56,320 --> 00:05:56,630
Example two.

124
00:06:00,010 --> 00:06:03,130
Yeah, to be honest, I do struggle with time management.

125
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:07,970
Example three.

126
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:15,820
The teacher farted in class and I really had to struggle not to laugh.

127
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:19,800
Okay.

128
00:06:20,690 --> 00:06:21,550
So, great examples.

129
00:06:22,450 --> 00:06:24,470
Let's move on to our next word.

130
00:06:25,110 --> 00:06:25,350
Punctuality.

131
00:06:26,010 --> 00:06:26,570
Punctuality.

132
00:06:27,450 --> 00:06:28,170
Punctuality.

133
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:29,200
Punctuality.

134
00:06:30,010 --> 00:06:30,210
Okay.

135
00:06:30,290 --> 00:06:31,850
So, to be punctual.

136
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:34,710
It means to be on time, always.

137
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:36,600
Always.

138
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:38,110
So, punctuality would be the noun form.

139
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:39,520
Right.

140
00:06:39,520 --> 00:06:43,190
So, the condition or the state of always being on time.

141
00:06:43,800 --> 00:06:44,090
Right.

142
00:06:44,510 --> 00:06:47,230
So, punctuality, to be punctual.

143
00:06:47,240 --> 00:06:48,240
Yeah.

144
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:50,810
And our interviewee struggles with punctuality.

145
00:06:51,150 --> 00:06:51,350
Right.

146
00:06:51,590 --> 00:06:51,870
Like me.

147
00:06:52,550 --> 00:06:55,170
I guess you guys have something in common.

148
00:06:55,470 --> 00:06:55,830
We do.

149
00:06:56,510 --> 00:07:03,260
Now, he has a problem with punctuality, but he said he has addressed this weakness.

150
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:04,620
Yes, to address.

151
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:05,460
Address.

152
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:07,160
Address.

153
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:10,400
Now, if you notice, it's spelled the same way as address.

154
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:11,180
That's right.

155
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:12,940
But you pronounce it differently.

156
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:17,680
So, the verb address has the stress on the second syllable.

157
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:19,280
Okay.

158
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:20,280
Address.

159
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:21,280
Yes.

160
00:07:21,280 --> 00:07:22,280
And that means to...

161
00:07:22,280 --> 00:07:23,280
Deal with.

162
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:24,000
To deal with the problem.

163
00:07:24,280 --> 00:07:25,280
Yeah.

164
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:25,880
To acknowledge the problem.

165
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:27,280
Yeah.

166
00:07:27,280 --> 00:07:27,890
And to try and solve it.

167
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:29,280
And to try and solve it.

168
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:30,280
Okay.

169
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:31,280
So, different from your home address where you live.

170
00:07:31,280 --> 00:07:32,280
Yeah.

171
00:07:32,280 --> 00:07:34,280
And there, the stress is on the first syllable.

172
00:07:34,280 --> 00:07:35,280
Address.

173
00:07:35,280 --> 00:07:36,280
Right.

174
00:07:36,280 --> 00:07:37,280
Perfect.

175
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:38,280
Very good.

176
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:39,280
Okay.

177
00:07:39,280 --> 00:07:43,440
So, let's listen to this dialogue one more time, and then we'll come back and talk about these two questions.

178
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:45,610
Greatest weakness and why should we hire you?

179
00:07:54,720 --> 00:07:54,950
Very good.

180
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:57,880
So, I have a couple of final questions.

181
00:07:57,990 --> 00:07:58,850
I hope they're not too hard.

182
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:05,640
Well, why should we hire you?

183
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:07,730
I think that I would be a perfect fit in this company.

184
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:13,090
I have a unique combination of strong technical acumen with outstanding soft skills.

185
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:17,110
I excel at building strong, long-term consumer relationships.

186
00:08:17,960 --> 00:08:24,040
For example, when I headed the customer support department in my previous company, our team resolved about 70% of our customers' problems.

187
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,100
I decided that we needed better information and technical preparation on our products.

188
00:08:29,860 --> 00:08:34,420
So after, I implemented a series of training sessions in coordination with our technical department.

189
00:08:34,960 --> 00:08:37,360
We were able to solve 90% of our customers' issues.

190
00:08:37,920 --> 00:08:41,460
Given the opportunity, I could bring this kind of success to this company.

191
00:08:42,520 --> 00:08:43,840
Impressive.

192
00:08:45,950 --> 00:08:48,890
So, what would you consider to be your greatest weakness?

193
00:08:49,430 --> 00:08:53,110
To be honest, I struggle with organization and time management.

194
00:08:53,960 --> 00:08:56,080
Punctuality has never been a strength of mine.

195
00:08:56,080 --> 00:08:57,750
I find it hard to organize my time effectively.

196
00:08:58,330 --> 00:09:03,830
I've actually addressed this weakness recently by attending a workshop on efficient time management.

197
00:09:04,410 --> 00:09:10,490
It helped me a lot by providing me with great insights on how to get organized and use my time efficiently.

198
00:09:10,850 --> 00:09:12,390
So, I think I'm getting better now.

199
00:09:13,960 --> 00:09:15,440
Great.

200
00:09:18,040 --> 00:09:21,380
Well, let me tell you that I am very pleased with this interview.

201
00:09:22,060 --> 00:09:23,880
We are shortlisting our candidates this week.

202
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:30,920
And next week, we will inform our shortlisted candidates of the day and time for a second interview with our CEO.

203
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:32,180
Great.

204
00:09:32,460 --> 00:09:32,860
Thanks a lot.

205
00:09:33,300 --> 00:09:34,160
I hope to hear from you.

206
00:09:34,560 --> 00:09:34,680
Goodbye.

207
00:09:38,480 --> 00:09:43,320
All right, Marco.

208
00:09:43,570 --> 00:09:46,850
Well, this classic question, why should we hire you?

209
00:09:46,920 --> 00:09:49,860
I think you'll hear it in many, many different interviews.

210
00:09:49,860 --> 00:09:51,560
And how did he deal with it here?

211
00:09:51,560 --> 00:10:01,690
Well, the first thing that he said is very, very important because they're asking you why so your immediate reaction would be to answer with because or I think that.

212
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:06,030
But there's a different way of approaching it, which is a little bit more professional.

213
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:10,410
He said, I think that I would be a perfect fit in this company.

214
00:10:10,760 --> 00:10:12,810
I think I would be a perfect fit in this company.

215
00:10:13,070 --> 00:10:14,450
That's how he started his answer.

216
00:10:14,590 --> 00:10:15,850
And then he explained why.

217
00:10:16,350 --> 00:10:18,450
So this sounds really strong, doesn't it?

218
00:10:18,690 --> 00:10:25,670
Yeah, basically, it just makes you seem to be very confident and puts you like you are already part of the company.

219
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:29,220
So this is a great way to start off this question.

220
00:10:29,220 --> 00:10:30,790
But you got to follow this up with some information.

221
00:10:31,300 --> 00:10:32,950
So what kind of information should you be giving?

222
00:10:33,570 --> 00:10:37,550
Well, like we talked about in your work experience, the same thing goes here.

223
00:10:38,110 --> 00:10:40,570
What do you have to offer to this company?

224
00:10:40,870 --> 00:10:47,190
What special skills or insights or experience can you bring that would help us?

225
00:10:47,450 --> 00:10:48,970
Yeah, that nobody else can bring.

226
00:10:49,230 --> 00:10:49,430
Exactly.

227
00:10:50,360 --> 00:10:57,040
So again, you have to start talking about your strengths and your previous experience, but with examples, right?

228
00:10:57,040 --> 00:10:58,170
Very, very specific examples.

229
00:10:58,520 --> 00:11:05,360
And whenever you can throw in some numbers or some specific successes that you have had.

230
00:11:05,360 --> 00:11:06,360
Exactly.

231
00:11:06,360 --> 00:11:19,040
And once you've talked about these things, you can close off this answer by saying, given the opportunity, I could bring this kind of success to this company.

232
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:22,390
Given the opportunity, I could bring this kind of success to this company.

233
00:11:23,080 --> 00:11:31,950
So that is just like a magical sentence saying like, if you give me the chance, I will demonstrate how good I am and how I can help this company.

234
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:33,280
Yes.

235
00:11:33,280 --> 00:11:35,870
A really, really strong closing argument.

236
00:11:36,640 --> 00:11:37,640
Yes.

237
00:11:37,640 --> 00:11:41,270
So that's the way you should more or less approach that question, right?

238
00:11:41,630 --> 00:11:45,910
All right, but let's turn now to the classic, classic question.

239
00:11:46,680 --> 00:11:47,250
Your greatest weakness.

240
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:48,650
What is your greatest weakness?

241
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:55,830
When you get this question, be sure to not say, my greatest weakness is I work too much.

242
00:11:56,140 --> 00:11:58,430
My greatest weakness is I'm a workaholic.

243
00:11:58,840 --> 00:11:59,010
Right.

244
00:11:59,430 --> 00:12:00,470
Or what was it?

245
00:12:00,670 --> 00:12:02,750
Oh, or I am a perfectionist.

246
00:12:02,850 --> 00:12:04,990
Yeah, these we can see right through these.

247
00:12:05,190 --> 00:12:08,190
I mean, they're too obvious and they're too, they're too silly.

248
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:12,880
I mean, everyone has a weakness, so don't try and turn it around into a strength.

249
00:12:12,880 --> 00:12:14,680
I just don't think that's a good idea.

250
00:12:14,680 --> 00:12:15,680
Okay.

251
00:12:15,680 --> 00:12:19,550
So yeah, be sure to stay away from these answers, but you can say anything.

252
00:12:19,960 --> 00:12:22,790
Like in this case, he has a problem with being on time.

253
00:12:23,240 --> 00:12:24,240
Yeah.

254
00:12:24,240 --> 00:12:27,510
So be honest, say what one of your greatest weaknesses are.

255
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:35,810
But the key to making this weakness look good is saying what you are doing to solve this weakness.

256
00:12:36,760 --> 00:12:37,760
Exactly.

257
00:12:37,760 --> 00:12:39,150
So really be truthful.

258
00:12:40,720 --> 00:12:45,050
Tell the interviewer what you're not good at because there's obviously something that you're not good at.

259
00:12:45,460 --> 00:12:46,460
Exactly.

260
00:12:46,460 --> 00:12:50,280
So you can say, I am usually late, but I am getting more organized.

261
00:12:50,280 --> 00:12:52,960
I am doing the seminar.

262
00:12:52,960 --> 00:12:56,000
Let the interviewer know that you are trying to improve this weakness.

263
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:57,000
Exactly.

264
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,270
And be sure to also think about your strength, right?

265
00:12:59,610 --> 00:12:59,750
Yeah.

266
00:12:59,830 --> 00:13:03,010
Be prepared to answer the question, what is your greatest strength?

267
00:13:04,110 --> 00:13:11,330
So and again, when you're talking about your strengths, be sure it's something more specific than saying, oh, I'm very responsible.

268
00:13:11,850 --> 00:13:12,090
Yeah.

269
00:13:12,650 --> 00:13:15,430
You want to be as specific as you can.

270
00:13:15,590 --> 00:13:19,650
And again, give those examples, give those successes, give those numbers.

271
00:13:20,280 --> 00:13:21,280
Right.

272
00:13:21,280 --> 00:13:22,280
Exactly.

273
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:23,640
So think about your weaknesses.

274
00:13:23,640 --> 00:13:24,350
Think about your strengths.

275
00:13:25,120 --> 00:13:30,010
If you don't know them, ask people, ask your colleagues or ask your friends, what do you think is my greatest strength?

276
00:13:30,230 --> 00:13:31,270
Maybe you're a funny person.

277
00:13:31,450 --> 00:13:31,830
That's a strength.

278
00:13:32,150 --> 00:13:32,350
Yes.

279
00:13:33,600 --> 00:13:40,200
Well, remember there are three parts to this interviewing series and you can find them on our website at EnglishPod.com.

280
00:13:40,530 --> 00:13:40,750
Right.

281
00:13:41,280 --> 00:13:43,400
Come to our website EnglishPod.com.

282
00:13:43,400 --> 00:13:47,250
You can look at all of these three lessons and I hope they are really helpful.

283
00:13:47,680 --> 00:13:54,520
And of course, if you have any questions or comments or maybe you'd like a little bit more of this interview series, let us know.

284
00:13:54,990 --> 00:13:55,390
All right, guys.

285
00:13:55,670 --> 00:13:56,350
Well, thanks for listening.

286
00:13:56,830 --> 00:13:58,890
Good luck in your interviews and until next time.

287
